+# Background processes
+
+Managed long-lived processes for the coding agent: start them, page their logs, stop the whole tree, and let them wake the agent. Processes belong to the running pi process (they survive `/reload`, `/new`, `/fork`, and tree navigation) and are stopped with SIGTERM-then-SIGKILL when pi exits.
+
+Load with `pi -e packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/background-processes` or copy this directory to `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` / `.pi/extensions/` so `DefaultResourceLoader` picks up `index.ts`.
+
+## Tools
+
+Every tool returns a JSON object as the result text and the same object as `details`. `bash` is the exception on its normal (foreground) path: it returns the built-in bash result text, truncation, and tool-error strings.
+
+| Tool | Arguments | Result |
+|------|-----------|--------|
+| `bg_run` | `command`, optional `cwd`, `name`, `wake.ready`, `wake.error`, `cleanup.command`, `cleanup.timeoutSec` | Process record (returns immediately) |
+| `bg_logs` | `id`, optional `offset`, `limit` | `{ id, offset, lines, total }` — a page of combined stdout/stderr. A page never exceeds 64KB. Omit `limit` to get the newest lines that fit. |
+| `bg_list` | _(none)_ | `{ processes }` — every record visible to this session, running and finished |
+| `bg_kill` | `id`, optional `timeoutSec` (default 5) | Final process record plus `cleanup: { ran, exitCode, failed } \| null` |
+| `bg_watch` | `id`, optional `ready`, `error`, `exit`, `output` | Updated process record |
+| `bash` | `command`, optional `timeout`, `stalledSec` (default 30) | Built-in bash result, or a process record with `backgrounded: true` and `stalledSec` if the command went silent while still running |
+
+A process record includes `id`, `name`, `command`, `cwd` (absolute), `pid`, `state` (`running` \| `exited` \| `killed`), `exitCode`, `startedAt`, `endedAt`, `logLines`, and `backgrounded` (true only when `bash` moved the command).
+
+Relative `cwd` values resolve against the session working directory. `bg_kill` signals the process and every descendant (`SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL` after `timeoutSec`). If the process was started with `cleanup`, that command runs once the tree is gone (same cwd, default 30s timeout) and its output is appended to the same log. A failed or timed-out cleanup is returned as `failed: true`.
+
+Logs and the final record are stored under `{sessionDir}/background-processes/{sessionId}/{id}/`. A later pi process that resumes the session can list and page finished records; leftover `running` files are reported as `killed`/`exited`, never `running`. Output is written to disk as it arrives so pi's heap does not grow with log volume.
+
+## Wake rules
+
+Wakes are custom messages with `customType: "background-process"` and `details: { id, name, reasons, exitCode, matchedLine }`. `reasons` is a non-empty list drawn from `"ready"`, `"error"`, `"output"`, `"exit"`. `matchedLine` is the first output line that triggered the wake, or `null`.
+
+- **Default:** only process exit wakes the agent.
+- **`ready` / `error`:** only when that regex was supplied (to `bg_run` or `bg_watch`). Patterns are matched against each complete output line.
+- **`output`:** only for a command that `bash` moved to the background. The first complete line after the switch wakes, with that line as `matchedLine`.
+- **`ready` and `output`** wake at most once per process. **`error`** wakes at most once per delivery window. **`exit`** wakes exactly once when the process leaves `running`, including when `bg_kill` stops it (`state: "killed"`).
+- After the exit wake has been delivered, nothing else wakes for that process. `bg_watch` with `null` removes a pattern; `exit: false` / `output: false` silences that reason.
+- While the agent is busy, the wake is delivered after the current assistant turn finishes its tool calls and before the next model request (`deliverAs: "steer"`). While idle, it starts a new turn immediately (`triggerTurn: true`).
+- Several reasons for the same process in one window may be combined into one message. Several processes are reported in the order each first fired.
+- The wake goes to the session that started the process if that session is still active; otherwise to the current active session.
